    "title": "New literacy initiative in Niagara Falls - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Mark Ludwiczak ,\n1 days ago\nBUFFALO, N.Y. (WIVB) — A new partnership between the Niagara Falls School District and the Niagara Falls Public Library is working to encourage children and families to read more.\nAt Abate Elementary School on Tuesday, a character-based performance by “Checkers the Inventor” showed how students would be encouraged to learn through reading. On Saturday, the library will host a community meet and greet so families can learn more about the new program.\n“I know that there are a variety of programs that our district already deploys to assist our students in reading, but when I started to see this particular approach, my immediate call was to the superintendent,” said Niagara Falls Mayor Robert Restaino. “It became clear to me that there are some of our students, at particular different grade levels, that might find this vehicle to be something that will entice them to read.”\nLatest Local News Sabres playoff excitement comes with increased risk for ticket scams Seneca Babcock CEO says services are up and running 1 dead after Potomac Avenue fire USA Hockey girls national championship kicks off in WNY Friends remember JohnLuc Jubert, killed in fatal car crash ***\nMark Ludwiczak joined the News 4 team in 2024. He is a veteran journalist with two decades of experience in Buffalo. You can follow him online at @marklud12 .\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to News 4 Buffalo.\n1",
    "summary": "BUFFALO, N.Y. (WIVB) — A new partnership between the Niagara Falls School District and the Niagara Falls Public Library is working to encourage children and families to read more. At Abate Elementary School on Tuesday, a character-based performance by “Checkers the Inventor” showed how students would be encouraged to learn through reading. On Saturday, the library will host a community meet and greet so families can learn more about the new program.",
